There are two groups of concepts in question:
    1. Visibility modifiers: public, private, protected and internal
    2. The inner keyword: This controls how a nested class relates to an object of its outer class


Assuming a hospital management system for our examples:


==================================================================
PART 1: KOTLIN VISIBILITY MODIFIERS
==================================================================
Imagine you are building a software for a hospital. Some information should be accessible to anyone,
some only inside a class and some only within a particular module.

Visibility modifiers let you to control that access.

============
1) PUBLIC
============
public is Kotlin's default visibility for most declarations.

    ```
        class MedicalDevice{
                public val deviceName ="Heart Monitor"
                public fun displayName(){
                    println($deviceName)
               }
       }

    fun main(){
        val device= MedicalDevice()
        println(device.deviceName)
        device.displayName()
    }

    ```


============
2) PRIVATE
============
private hides implementational detail from code that shouldn't access them.It is only accessible in the class
and via the functions that expose it.

```
    class MedicalDevice{
        private var secretCode= "HD-456"

        fun verifyCode(code: String): Boolean{
            return code== secretCode
        }
    }

    fun main(){
        val device= MedicalDevice()
        println(device.verifyCode("HD-456")) //true

        //println( device.secretCode) // Compilation error
    }

=============
3) PROTECTED
=============
protected items are only accessible in the class and its subclasses

```
        open class MedicalDevice {

            protected var isOperational = false

            protected fun runDiagnostic() {
                println("Running device diagnostic...")
            }
        }

        class HeartRateMonitor : MedicalDevice() {

            fun performSelfTest() {
                runDiagnostic()
                isOperational = true

                println("Self-test completed.")
            }
        }

        fun main() {
            val monitor = HeartRateMonitor()

            monitor.performSelfTest()

            // println(monitor.isOperational) // Compilation error
            // monitor.runDiagnostic()        // Compilation error
        }

```


=============
4) INTERNAL
=============
internal makes the thing accessible throughout the same module

```
        internal class DeviceDiagnostics {

            internal fun runDiagnostics() {
                println("Running internal diagnostics...")
            }
        }

        fun main() {
            val diagnostics = DeviceDiagnostics()
            diagnostics.runDiagnostics()
        }

```

A module can contain many Kotlin files, classes, interfaces, and functions. The important thing is that the code belongs to the same compilation unit.

Let's use your Smart Hospital project to understand this.
